H["Physiotherapy"] = "Direct billing to insurers is the deciding factor for most bookings and it belongs on the page, not in a phone call."
H["Pelvic health physiotherapy"] = "A sensitive category with high demand and low awareness, where discreet, specific content outperforms general clinic marketing."
H["Concussion and vestibular rehab"] = "Referral from sport organisations and physicians, with return-to-play protocols as the content that establishes credibility."
H["Chiropractic"] = "A crowded local category where new patient offers have trained the market to shop, and clinics competing on availability hold better margins."
H["Registered massage therapy"] = "Booked around insurance coverage limits, which makes remaining-benefit reminders near year end unusually effective."
H["Occupational therapy"] = "Funded through insurers, workplace claims and home care contracts, so the buyer is frequently a case manager rather than a patient."
H["Speech-language pathology"] = "Parents searching after a school or physician flag, with waitlist length as the deciding factor between clinics."
H["Kinesiology and athletic therapy"] = "Often not covered by insurance, which means the value has to be explained rather than assumed."
H["Naturopathic medicine"] = "Advertising claims are tightly constrained by regulatory colleges, and describing the assessment process is safer and more effective than describing outcomes."
H["Osteopathy"] = "Regulatory status and coverage vary by province and state, which changes both the messaging and what can be claimed."
H["Acupuncture and TCM"] = "Coverage questions dominate the first call, and clinics that publish which insurers they bill directly book more first visits."
H["Dietitians and clinical nutrition"] = "Registered dietitians compete for search visibility with unregulated nutrition advice, so credentials need to be prominent and explained."
H["Podiatry and chiropody"] = "Custom orthotics carry the margin and coverage questions drive the booking, so the two need to be answered together."
H["Orthotics and prosthetics"] = "Long-term patient relationships funded through insurance and public programs, where the funding pathway is the content that matters."
H["Multidisciplinary rehab clinics"] = "Motor vehicle accident and workplace claim work brings a referral audience of adjusters and lawyers alongside patients."

# --- Mental and behavioural health (11) ---
H["Psychology"] = "Session cost and coverage are the first questions, and practices that publish their rate get fewer calls and more bookings."
H["Counselling and therapy practices"] = "Matching a client to the right therapist inside the practice is where most enquiries are lost, not at the ad."
H["Group and multi-clinician practices"] = "Individual therapist profiles with real specialisation detail convert better than any practice-level page."
H["Addiction and recovery centres"] = "Advertising is restricted and, in the United States, subject to certification requirements for treatment providers."
H["Eating disorder programs"] = "Platform policy prohibits most weight and body imagery, which removes the obvious creative approach entirely."
H["Ketamine and TMS clinics"] = "New treatment categories where patients arrive uncertain what is legitimate, so evidence and physician oversight are the content."
H["Autism services and ABA therapy"] = "Funding pathways differ by province and state, and the funding question is what parents are actually searching."
H["Behavioural health networks"] = "Multi-site operations where clinician availability by location changes weekly and the website almost never reflects it."
H["Employee assistance providers"] = "Sold to HR leaders on utilisation and reporting, so it is a business to business program with a consumer-facing surface."
H["Telehealth and virtual care"] = "Licensing is jurisdictional, so advertising has to be geographically constrained to where the clinicians can legally practise."
H["Digital mental health apps"] = "App store economics and health claims restrictions collide, which limits what performance creative can say."

# --- Aesthetics and med spa (9) ---
H["Med spas"] = "Membership and treatment plans hold margin where single-session discounting destroys it, and the difference shows within two quarters."
H["Injectables and botox clinics"] = "Per-unit pricing is compared openly, and clinics competing on price alone lose clients to whoever discounts next."
H["Laser and skin clinics"] = "Device brand names carry real search volume, and pages built around the specific technology outrank general treatment pages."
H["Body contouring"] = "Meta prohibits ads implying negative self-perception about appearance, which rules out most of the copy this category defaults to."
H["Hair restoration and transplant"] = "Results imagery is restricted, so surgeon credentials, technique explanation and consultation process carry the decision."
H["Tattoo removal"] = "Session count and total cost are the questions, and clinics that publish a realistic range get better qualified consultations."
H["Teeth whitening studios"] = "Competing against at-home kits on price, so the argument is supervised results and speed rather than cost."
H["Permanent makeup and lash studios"] = "Artist-led rather than brand-led, where the individual technician's portfolio drives bookings."
H["Medical aesthetics training"] = "The buyer is a practitioner making a career investment, so the content is about certification, earning potential and course logistics."

# --- Wellness and longevity (10) ---
H["IV therapy"] = "Claims about benefits are the compliance risk, and clinics that describe the protocol rather than the outcome clear review reliably."
H["Hormone therapy and men's health"] = "Heavily restricted advertising with rising enforcement, where the safe position is describing testing and physician oversight."
H["Menopause and midlife care"] = "Underserved demand with high search volume and very little quality content, which makes organic search unusually winnable."
H["Weight loss and GLP-1 programs"] = "The most scrutinised advertising category in healthcare right now, with restrictions tightening across every platform."
H["Preventative and longevity clinics"] = "Self-pay programs at high price points where publishing what is included and what it costs is the entire differentiator."
H["Integrative and functional medicine"] = "Testing panels and protocol detail are what prospective patients compare, and vague wellness language loses to specifics."
H["Wellness clinics"] = "A broad label that means nothing to a searcher, so the pages that work are built around the specific service rather than the category."
H["Cryotherapy and recovery studios"] = "Membership economics decide the business, so the marketing goal is the trial that converts to a recurring plan."
H["Sauna and float studios"] = "Local, experiential and membership driven, where first visit conversion to membership is the only metric that matters."
H["Sexual wellness clinics"] = "Discretion drives the format, and anonymous enquiry options convert substantially better than standard contact forms."

# --- Animal health (11) ---
H["Veterinary clinics"] = "Many practices are at capacity and closed to new patients, which makes recruitment marketing more valuable than acquisition."
H["Animal hospitals and emergency vet"] = "Emergency searches happen overnight, and publishing the current wait time and intake status prevents wasted trips and the bad reviews that follow them."
H["Veterinary specialty and referral"] = "The audience is referring general practice vets, so the marketing is business to business and the content is clinical."
H["Mobile and house-call vets"] = "Service area accuracy is the whole campaign, because a booking outside the radius costs an hour of unpaid driving."
H["Equine and large animal vets"] = "Rural, relationship driven and low search volume, where a well-built page can own the category in a region outright."
H["Exotic and avian vets"] = "Owners travel long distances for species-specific expertise, so the pages should be built per species rather than per service."
H["Veterinary dentistry"] = "Owners do not know the service exists until a vet raises it, which makes the content job education rather than acquisition."
H["Pet wellness and grooming"] = "Recurring appointments where booking convenience and consistency of groomer decide retention."
H["Pet boarding and daycare"] = "Demand spikes around holidays and the booking happens weeks ahead, so campaign timing is the main lever."
H["Pet insurance"] = "A comparison-shopped financial product where exclusions and waiting periods are what buyers actually compare."
H["Pet telehealth"] = "Licensing rules restrict what can be diagnosed remotely, which varies by jurisdiction and constrains the advertising claim."

# --- Pharmacy, labs and diagnostics (8) ---
H["Pharmacies"] = "Competing with chains on hours and location, where services like injections and medication reviews are the differentiator worth advertising."
H["Compounding pharmacies"] = "Prescriber relationships drive volume, so the marketing audience is physicians and veterinarians rather than patients."
H["Specialty and infusion pharmacy"] = "Payer contracts and clinic relationships decide the business, and consumer marketing has almost no role."
H["Online and DTC pharmacy"] = "Prescription advertising rules differ sharply between Canada and the United States, and getting it wrong is a licensing problem."
H["Medical laboratories"] = "Turnaround time and report clarity are what referring clinicians choose on, and neither is usually on the website."
H["Diagnostic imaging and radiology"] = "Public wait times push patients toward private imaging, and publishing your next available appointment is the strongest message available."
H["Dental and medical labs"] = "Business to business with practices as clients, where case turnaround and remake rates are the buying criteria."
H["At-home and consumer testing"] = "Regulated as both a health service and a consumer product, and the claims permitted are narrower than most brands assume."

